; The Kestrel (GAMEPLAY-DESIGN P3.3): the all-rounder, and the frame axis's own
; origin — P3.4 defines its whole column as zeros ("the frame you fly when intel
; is stale"), so the balance instrument bands every other frame as a delta
; against it.
;
; evasion_style "jink" is stated rather than left to the default, because a
; roster's evasion identity should be readable in the roster's own files. It
; flies the PILOT_VERSION 5 tactical jink — break, settle, fire, break — which is
; measured as best on both axes at once for this airframe (v1.81), and which the
; Atlas is measured as unable to fly at all.
;
; NO `component_armor`, and the absence is authored rather than skipped
; (Iteration 17 / E4.2). It is E4.2's own worked example — *"a 650 g quad carries
; nothing"* — and it is also what this frame IS: P3.4 defines the Kestrel's whole
; column as zeros on purpose, so the datum stays the thing every other frame is a
; delta against. A plated Kestrel would move the origin of the ruler.
[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="FrameConfig" load_steps=3 format=3]

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://resources/frame_config.gd" id="1_frame"]
[ext_resource type="Resource" path="res://resources/default_flight_kestrel.tres" id="2_flight"]

[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_frame")
frame_id = &"kestrel"
display_name = "Kestrel"
flight_config = ExtResource("2_flight")
hull = 100.0
evasion_style = &"jink"
